# Flutter toolchain (fvm)

- Always use **`./flutterw`** (Unix/macOS/Linux) or **`./flutterw.cmd`** (Windows) instead of a global `flutter`. The wrappers run `fvm flutter` against the version in `.fvmrc`.
- For Dart commands (`dart run`, `dart format`, codegen, etc.), use **`./dartw`** or **`./dartw.cmd`** (`fvm dart`).
- FVM must be installed globally. If a wrapper says it is missing, install it from https://fvm.app.

Run wrapper commands from **`client/`** (or use explicit paths like `client/flutterw` from the repo root).

```bash
cd client
./flutterw pub get
./flutterw pub run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs
./flutterw analyze    # lint
./flutterw build web  # build for web
```

# Riverpod architecture

- Use Riverpod for shared cross-route state, asynchronous domain state, dependency injection, keyed state families, and resources that need lifecycle cleanup. Keep animation, focus, temporary form values, and other widget-local UI state in the widget.
- For new mutable state, use Riverpod 3 `Notifier`/`AsyncNotifier`; do not add new `StateNotifier`, `StateNotifierProvider`, or `flutter_riverpod/legacy.dart` usage. Migrate existing legacy providers only as focused work.
- Keep domain services and controllers independent of Riverpod. Resolve dependencies in the provider layer and pass explicit constructor arguments; never pass `Ref` or `WidgetRef` into a domain object.
- In widgets, `watch` render state, `listen` for UI side effects, and `read` notifiers only from event handlers or lifecycle coordination. Do not use `ProviderScope.containerOf` when a `WidgetRef` can be passed explicitly.
- Every family must make its lifetime intentional: prefer `autoDispose` for route/entity-scoped state; document why a family is kept alive and ensure subscriptions, timers, and other resources are disposed.
- Place providers in feature-specific state files. Do not add new cross-feature barrel exports or screen-local providers; import the defining feature file directly when adding new code.
- Providers should expose state, dependencies, or derived values. Put startup sequencing and one-shot side effects in an explicit coordinator initialized at the composition root, with ownership and disposal visible.
- This project uses hand-written providers. Do not introduce Riverpod annotation/codegen ad hoc; changing provider-generation strategy requires a deliberate project-wide decision.
- Use provider overrides as test seams for infrastructure. Prefer one documented `ProviderContainer`/`UncontrolledProviderScope` harness pattern over bespoke setup.
- Treat `riverpod_lint` diagnostics as required analyzer feedback. Do not suppress them without a specific documented reason.
